================================================================================
 ai.wing — CHANGELOG & BUILD LOG
 Aloha Fiber — Independent Authorized Retailer of Aloha Broadband
================================================================================

Format: newest entry at the top. Every structural change, feature addition and
component creation is logged here for version tracking.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v3.0.0] — STATIC HTML EXPORT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The whole site now builds to plain HTML in ./out, deployable to any static
  host with no Node process. `npm run build` produces it.

## HOW, AND WHY NOTHING WAS LOST

  next.config.mjs -> output: 'export', trailingSlash: true.

  Nothing had to be rewritten or reimplemented. Every effect on this site is
  either CSS or client-side JavaScript, and a static export ships both
  unchanged — the same stylesheet and the same React bundle, just without a
  server rendering pages on request. The alternative (hand-porting eight
  interactive components to vanilla JS) would have guaranteed drift; this
  guarantees parity.

  Pre-flight confirmed the site had no server dependencies at all:
    0 API routes · 0 server actions · 0 next/image · 0 headers/redirects/
    rewrites · the one dynamic route already had generateStaticParams with
    dynamicParams = false.

  `trailingSlash: true` so pages emit as `privacy/index.html` rather than
  `privacy.html` — works from a filesystem root on hosts that do not rewrite
  extensionless URLs.

## OUTPUT

  10 real pages + 404, 3.1 MB total:
    index.html, privacy/, disclaimer/, cookies/, tcpa/, trademarks/,
    marketing-policy/, service-fulfillment/, pci-dss/, 404
    + _next/ (1.0 MB hashed CSS/JS) + images/ (588 KB)

## VERIFIED AGAINST THE EXPORT, NOT THE DEV SERVER

  Served ./out over a plain static file server and tested there:

    React hydrated ......... yes (event handlers attached, not inert markup)
    Lenis smooth scroll .... mounted
    html.js gate ........... present (progressive-enhancement gate intact)
    Running animations ..... 31
    Grain overlay .......... present
    Mesh gradient fields ... 24
    Ken Burns .............. 1     Marquee ............ 1
    Beam borders ........... 2     Spotlight cards .... 6
    Tilt cards ............. 3     Reveals ............ 38
    Wipes .................. 4     Photographs ........ 4
    ZIP checker ............ hit / miss / invalid all correct
    FAQ accordion .......... React state + class toggle correct
    All 8 legal pages ...... 200, full header/footer/aurora/grain
    Dev artifacts .......... none (no HMR client, no react-refresh)

## CHANGES MADE FOR THE EXPORT

  - package.json: `next start` cannot serve a static export, it expects a
    server build. `npm start` now serves ./out instead (via npx serve, so no
    dependency was added). `npm run preview` does the same.

  - app/layout.tsx: metadataBase now reads NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL, defaulting to
    the https://example.com placeholder. The domain is baked into every
    exported HTML file as the og:image base and CANNOT be changed after export
    without rebuilding, so it has to be set at build time:

        NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://yourdomain.com npm run build

    Verified both paths: with the variable set the real domain appears in the
    HTML; without it the placeholder does.

  - public/images/README.md moved to design/IMAGE-ASSETS.md. Anything under
    public/ ships, and build documentation should not be published.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v2.4.1] — HERO HEADLINE: HALO REMOVED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Reported as "not seen properly". At magnification the cause was obvious: a
  grey halo around every glyph.

  Source was the text-shadow I had been leaning on since the overlay was
  removed — `0 2px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.45)`. At a 16px blur radius a shadow does
  not crisp type, it paints a diffuse cloud behind it. Against the pale
  overcast sky in the photograph that cloud reads as dirt on the letterforms.

  The underlying mistake was using a shadow to do a scrim's job. A shadow
  scales with the glyph outline, so making it strong enough to carry contrast
  across a large headline necessarily makes it wide enough to be seen.

  Fix:
    - Every text-shadow removed from the hero headline and paragraph.
    - The neutral left scrim strengthened and extended instead:
        was  .66 -> .5 -> .24 -> clear by 62%
        now  .76 -> .68 -> .5 -> .24 -> .06 -> clear by 92%
      Still pure black, still adds no colour cast, and the photograph is still
      fully visible on the right where the copy does not sit.

  Type now renders crisp white with clean edges. Verified at 1024px and at
  390px mobile, where the copy spans the full width and the scrim's right end
  matters most.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v2.4.0] — SITE-WIDE: DEAD OPACITY CLASSES. WHY ALOHA: RANKED FIGURES.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## ROOT CAUSE FOUND — 14 OPACITY VALUES WERE EMITTING NO CSS AT ALL

  Tailwind's default opacity scale is sparse: 0,5,10,20,25,30,40,50,60,70,75,
  80,90,95,100. Any modifier off that scale silently emits NOTHING. No warning,
  no build failure — the element just renders without the border, tint or muted
  colour it was written to have.

  This codebase used 47 distinct opacity-modifier classes across 25 values.
  FOURTEEN of them were off-scale and therefore dead:

      12  15  18  22  35  45  52  55  62  68  72  78  85  97

  Consequences, sitewide and invisible-by-design:
    - Hairline rules written as `border-white/12` fell through to the base
      `* { border-color: azure-150/60 }` rule, so subtle dark-mode rules were
      rendering as pale blue-grey lines instead.
    - Muted body colours like `text-navy-700/68` produced no colour rule at
      all, so that text inherited the body ink instead of its intended tint.
    - Panel tints like `bg-navy-700/55` and `ring-white/12` simply did not
      exist.

  This is very likely a large part of why successive rounds of this build kept
  reading as unrefined despite the layout work: a meaningful share of the
  hairlines and tonal steps were never actually rendering.

  FIX — tailwind.config.ts now defines every integer 0-100 as a valid opacity
  value. JIT still only emits what is used, so it costs nothing. Verified by
  extracting all opacity classes from source and grepping the built CSS: every
  one now emits. (`border-azure-150/60` is the sole apparent miss and is a
  false positive — it is applied via @apply, so the class name never appears in
  the output.)

  Also fixed: `lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.15fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]` was not being
  generated, collapsing the heading to one column. Changed to the value pattern
  already proven elsewhere in the codebase.

  LESSON RECORDED: a Tailwind class that does not exist fails silently. When
  something "looks off" and the markup reads correct, check that the class
  actually emitted before redesigning the component. Several rounds of layout
  changes here were treating a symptom of this.

## WHY ALOHA — FIGURES RANKED

  Finally reviewed on screen rather than by measurement. A temporary route was
  added at /design-preview rendering the section alone, because a fresh
  navigation captures reliably where scrolling mid-page does not. Route deleted
  afterwards. Worth recreating for any future section-level design work.

  Seeing it confirmed the remaining fault: six equally weighted figures is a
  dashboard. Uniform cells also stranded dead space, since a short figure
  cannot fill a column sized for the longest.

  Now ranked:
    - The three that sell the service (99.99% uptime, 5-25 ms latency,
      unlimited data) run large and open across the full width, separated by
      vertical rules rather than boxed.
    - The remaining three drop to one compact line each.
    - The heading splits across two columns, so the top of the section is no
      longer a left-weighted block with empty space beside it.

  Verified on screen: heading 576/480px two-column, vertical rules rendering at
  rgba(255,255,255,.12), no horizontal overflow, build clean.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v2.3.0] — COUNT-UP REMOVED, FIGURE SCALE DIALLED BACK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## COUNT-UP REMOVED ENTIRELY

  components/ui/CountUp.tsx DELETED. Both usages replaced with the plain value.

  It shipped a screen full of "0.00%", "0 mi", "0 hrs" TWICE in user-facing
  screenshots. Two separate defects were already fixed in it (negative frames
  from unclamped rAF progress; a zero-seeded initial state) and it still failed,
  because the remaining failure is inherent to the pattern rather than a bug:
  a count-up necessarily renders zero for its opening frames, and six of them
  entering the viewport together means the whole section reads as zeros at once.

  On a page whose entire argument is reliability and uptime, a statistic that
  can momentarily display "0.00% uptime" is worse than no animation. The
  animation was decorative; the number is the product claim. Removed rather
  than patched a third time.

  Rule recorded in the component docblock: figures on this page are never
  animated from a placeholder value.

## FIGURE SCALE

  v2.2.0 overcorrected. Having diagnosed "not enough scale contrast", I set the
  figure at 46.4px, which swamped the cell — reported as "so big, not nice".

  Now 34.4px (down 26%), giving a 2.26x ratio over the claim line. Hierarchy
  comes from the GAP between levels, not from the size of the top one. Cell
  padding, icon size and internal rhythm tightened to match.

  Verified: all six figures render their real values immediately, no zero
  state at any point; type levels 34.4 / 15.2 / 10.9px.

  Still not visually confirmed — the Browser pane capture continues to fail
  while the window is backgrounded.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v2.2.0] — WHY ALOHA: HIERARCHY FIX (the actual problem)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Fourth attempt at this section, and the first one aimed at the right thing.

  DIAGNOSIS. Cards -> bento -> editorial index all failed identically because
  every one of them changed the CONTAINER and left the HIERARCHY alone. Each
  version stacked four type levels — stat, unit label, title, body — at close
  to the same size, so nothing dominated and the block read as a spec sheet no
  matter how it was boxed.

  Two further faults visible in the v2.1.0 screenshot:
    - The photograph was cropped 4:5. The source is a distant tower under a
      large sky, so a portrait crop kept mostly dead grey and read as a
      placeholder.
    - The feature glyphs sat in a right-hand column, creating a THIRD
      alignment that fought the two-column rhythm and left them floating
      unattached to any text.

  FIX — scale contrast, not another container:
    - The statistic is set at 46px against a 10.9px unit label and a 15.7px
      claim. A ~4.3x gap between the largest and smallest levels means the
      figure leads and the rest supports, which is what makes a stat block
      read as designed rather than tabulated.
    - Per-feature body copy dropped entirely (0 paragraphs per cell). That
      detail belongs in the FAQ, not stacked under a headline number.
    - Glyph moved inline above the figure, killing the third alignment column.
    - Photograph recropped to a 21:9 cinematic band at full section width,
      which uses the horizon instead of the empty sky, with the ownership
      claim set over its dark left third.
    - Separation is a 1px grid gap over a light parent, so the six cells read
      as one ruled table rather than six floating cards.
    - Figures count up on entry, reinforcing that the numbers are the subject.

  Verified: 3 columns, 1px gap, 6 cells, 0 <article> containers, 0 body
  paragraphs per cell, figure locked to 21:9, type levels 46.4 / 15.7 / 10.9px.

  NOT VISUALLY CONFIRMED. The Browser pane's capture failed repeatedly while
  the window was backgrounded, so this round was verified by measurement only.
  Needs an eye on it.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v2.1.0] — WHY ALOHA REBUILT AS AN EDITORIAL INDEX
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Reported again as generic. The v1.6.0 rebuild swapped a uniform card row for
  a bento grid, which was the wrong correction: the section shape was still
  eyebrow -> heading -> subhead -> CARD GRID -> CTA band. That shape is the
  generic marketing-page template regardless of how the cards are arranged. A
  bento grid is still a grid.

  Rebuilt on a different structural idea — a print-style feature index:

    LEFT (sticky)   The argument. Eyebrow, headline, one-line thesis, then the
                    tower photograph as a 4:5 portrait figure carrying the
                    single strongest claim in its caption. Stays in place while
                    the reasons scroll past it.

    RIGHT           The six reasons as ruled editorial rows. Oversized muted
                    index numerals (01-06), the statistic as display type with
                    its label in accent, then title and body. Separated by
                    hairline rules, not card borders.

  What was removed, deliberately:
    - Every card container. Zero <article> elements remain in the section.
    - Card borders, tile backgrounds, backdrop blurs, per-card spotlights.
      The rules and the numbering carry the structure instead.
    - The duplicated eyebrow. It read "Why Aloha Broadband" directly above the
      heading "Why customers choose Aloha Broadband"; the eyebrow is now
      "The network".

  Interaction is carried by the rows themselves rather than by card chrome: on
  hover the top rule draws itself across the full width (700ms), the index
  numeral warms from white/18 to accent, and the feature glyph lights.

  Verified: two-column 363px/501px at desktop with the rail computing
  position:sticky, 6 ruled rows, 0 card containers, indices 01-06 rendering,
  body copy at 11.04:1, figure locked to 4:5. At 375px it collapses to a single
  335px column with zero overflow and the glyph column drops out.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v2.0.0] — PREMIUM LAYER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Three rounds of incremental polish had not delivered the requested step
  change, so this is a deliberate build of the things that actually separate a
  brand-grade site from a competent one, rather than another pass of tweaks.

## NEW PRIMITIVES

  + components/ui/Grain.tsx
      A fixed film-grain layer over the entire page, as an inline SVG
      feTurbulence data URI — one small base64 string, no network request, no
      repaint on scroll. This is the cheapest single thing that stops flat
      digital gradients reading as synthetic.

  + components/ui/Aurora.tsx  (rewritten)
      Was three blurred circles that drifted. Now a four-field mesh gradient
      where each field drifts AND rotates hue (+/-20deg, so it never wanders
      off-brand). The fourth field fills the centre specifically so the effect
      does not resolve as "three blobs". 24 fields across the page.

  + components/ui/Spotlight.tsx
      A light that follows the pointer across a group of cards. ONE
      rAF-throttled listener per group writes --mx/--my to every card; each
      card paints its own highlight from those variables. Cost is constant
      regardless of card count. 12 cards wired: plans, features, fine print.

  + components/ui/CountUp.tsx
      Stats count up on entry. Operates on strings rather than numbers, so one
      component handles "99.99%", "5-25 ms", "20+ yrs" and "Unlimited" — it
      animates the first number found and leaves every other character in
      place. Decimal places are inferred from the source.

  + globals.css: .spotlight-layer and .beam-border
      beam-border is a conic gradient rotating behind the card, masked to a 1px
      ring. Used on exactly two elements — the featured plan and the closing
      CTA — because the effect stops reading as premium the moment it is
      everywhere.

## HERO

  - Mesh aurora raised to 0.95 intensity, blend opacity 60% -> 75%.
  - Added a slow-breathing coral accent orb on the horizon (9s cycle).
  - Stat strip figures now count up.

## THREE BUGS FOUND AND FIXED WHILE BUILDING THIS

  1. NEGATIVE STATISTICS. requestAnimationFrame hands the callback the FRAME'S
     start timestamp, which can predate a performance.now() captured moments
     earlier. That produced negative progress, and easeOutExpo turns negative
     progress into a negative number — the strip briefly painted "-7.18%" and
     "-1+ yrs". Progress is now clamped at both ends, not just the top.

  2. STATS COULD STICK AT ZERO. The first implementation seeded the display at
     0 on mount and relied on the observer to animate it up. In a hidden tab
     IntersectionObserver never fires at threshold 0.4, so the figure sat on
     screen reading "0.00%". Inverted: the display now starts at the REAL value
     and is only knocked to zero at the instant the animation begins. Every
     failure path — hidden tab, dead observer, unmount mid-flight — now leaves
     the correct number. A statistic must degrade to correct, never to zero.
     Backed by a settle timeout (setTimeout still fires when rAF is frozen) and
     a visibilitychange handler.

  3. BEAM RING WAS CLIPPED. `.beam-border::before` sat on the same element as
     `overflow-hidden` (needed to clip the card's ambient wash), so the ring
     was clipped away entirely. Moved onto a wrapper around the article.

  Also: .spotlight-layer needed z-index 0 + pointer-events none, and the
  fine-print card's header/dl needed `relative`, or the layer painted over the
  content instead of under it.

  Verified: 4/4 wipes open, 0/40 reveals faded, 0 overflowing elements, no
  horizontal scroll, no negative count-up frames across 30 samples, build
  clean.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v1.7.0] — FAKE PROMO FRAMING REMOVED, HERO GRADED, DUPLICATE TICKER CUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## THE CARRIER RUNS NO PROMOTIONS — CORRECTED

  Challenged on whether Aloha Broadband actually has offers. Re-audited: swept
  /, /pricing, /learn-more and /contact-us for promo / offer / special / deal /
  discount / save / limited time / free month / sale / % off / waive / credit.

  Result: ZERO real promotions. The only matches were the heading
  "What We Offer:" (a feature list) and the phrase "special antenna's".

  The hero had been showing a "NOW ON" pill over "30-day money-back guarantee /
  Unlimited data, zero usage caps". Those are STANDING TERMS of the service,
  not an active offer, and dressing them in urgency framing misrepresented the
  carrier. This was my error from the initial build.

  Fixes:
    - `promos` export renamed to `assurances`, with a provenance note recording
      the sweep and an explicit instruction never to add urgency treatment to
      them. If Aloha ever publishes a genuine promotion it goes in a separate
      `promos` export rather than reusing these.
    - Hero pill replaced with a standing fact about the operator: "Locally
      owned and operated in Kaʻū & Puna, Hawaiʻi Island for over 20 years."
    - The three assurances now sit as plain chips inside the availability card,
      presented as terms rather than as a deal.
    - Verified: no urgency language ("now on", "limited time", "hurry", "ends
      soon", "act now") anywhere in the rendered page.

## HERO — GRADED OUT OF DULL

  The supplied photograph is deliberately flat, low-contrast documentary
  overcast, which is why it read as dull. Two corrections, both in CSS so the
  source asset stays untouched and the grade is tunable in one place:

    - CSS filter on the photo layer: saturate(1.35) contrast(1.14)
      brightness(1.04).
    - A screen-blended warm horizon wash anchored at 78%/62% of the frame,
      running sunset orange -> coral -> azure -> clear. It adds light to the
      sea and sky and ties the frame to the brand accent instead of leaving it
      flat slate.

  Hero badge also restyled with a coral border and glow so it carries weight.

## DUPLICATE TICKER REMOVED

  The same Marquee, with the same items and the same styling, was running in
  both the hero stat strip and the Why Aloha CTA band — it read as one
  component pasted twice. Removed from Why Aloha; the hero keeps it, which
  still satisfies the brief's infinite-ticker requirement.
  Verified: exactly one marquee on the page.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v1.6.1] — HERO REGRESSION FIXES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Two defects introduced by v1.6.0, both reported and both reproduced.

  1. ACCENT HEADLINE RENDERED AS A DARK SMUDGE.
     v1.6.0 gave the accent words a `bg-clip-text text-transparent` gradient
     sheen while the parent h1 still carried a `text-shadow`. Those two do not
     compose: the shadow paints a solid dark silhouette BEHIND glyphs whose
     fill is a clipped gradient, so "Kaʻū and Puna." came out as a muddy brown
     blob instead of coral.
     Fix: dropped the gradient sheen, back to solid `text-sunset-400`.
     Verified computed colour is now rgb(255,75,51).

     Rule going forward: never put text-shadow on bg-clip-text/text-transparent.

  2. STAT STRIP APPEARED TO BLEED EDGE TO EDGE.
     The strip itself was correctly inset, but the hero <section> carried
     `bg-navy-800`. Below the photo panel, that background filled the full
     viewport width behind the strip, reading as a full-bleed band with the
     ticker floating in it.
     Fix: background moved off the section and onto the photo panel, so the
     section is transparent and the strip floats over the boundary onto the
     white section below — which is the effect that was intended.
     Verified: section background is rgba(0,0,0,0); at 1024px the card sits
     32->977 with the marquee fully inside it; at 375px it sits 20->355 and
     collapses to 2 columns. No horizontal overflow at either width.

  ALSO: the three-stop text shadow from v1.6.0 read as grey blobs around the
  glyphs. Reduced to a single clean stop, and recoloured from navy to neutral
  black so it darkens without tinting.

  LEFT VIGNETTE REINTRODUCED — with a caveat worth recording. The hero headline
  sits over pale overcast sky and was genuinely hard to read with no treatment
  at all. Added a NEUTRAL BLACK gradient confined to the left edge that is
  fully clear by 62% across. This is deliberately not the thing that was
  objected to: the previous overlay was a navy-blue wash across the whole
  frame that tinted the photograph. This one adds no colour and leaves the
  right two-thirds of the image untouched.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v1.6.0] — HERO OVERLAY REMOVED, MOTION SYSTEM, WHY ALOHA REBUILT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## HERO — OVERLAY REMOVED

  Both scrim layers deleted. The photograph now renders at full strength with
  no colour wash over it (verified: zero overlay divs remain in #top).

  Legibility is held by TYPE TREATMENT rather than a veil — a three-stop text
  shadow (tight 1-2px pair hugging the glyphs, plus a wide 38px soft pass) on
  the headline and paragraph. This was the trade-off of removing the scrim;
  flagged to the user.

## MOTION SYSTEM

  + components/ui/Aurora.tsx — three large blurred radial fields of official
    brand colour on long, offset, transform-only loops (26s / 32s / 38s).
    GPU-composited, never triggers layout. `tone` switches between navy and
    light sections, `intensity` scales alpha.

  New keyframes in tailwind.config.ts:
    kenburns  34s  slow scale+drift across the hero photograph
    auroraA   26s  drifting brand light
    auroraB   32s  counter-drifting brand light
    sheen      7s  gradient sweep across the accent words of a headline

  Applied: hero (ken burns on the photo, screen-blended aurora so it adds
  colour rather than veiling), plans, fine print, why-aloha, FAQ and footer.
  18 aurora fields across the page. All suppressed by the existing
  prefers-reduced-motion block.

  The hero headline accent is now a gradient-clipped animated sheen
  (#ff4b33 -> #ec6c27 -> #ff4b33) instead of flat coral.

## HERO — LAYOUT

  Added a stat strip (uptime / latency / data / years served) that OVERLAPS the
  hero's lower edge by -6rem and carries the benefit ticker along its base.
  The hero previously ended on a flat horizontal rule, which is the single
  biggest tell of a stock template block; an overlapping element breaks that
  edge and reads as a designed composition.

## WHY ALOHA — REBUILT

  The reported problems all traced to one decision: the tower photograph was a
  full-bleed section backdrop, which forced a heavy blue scrim, which in turn
  crushed the card contrast.

  - Full-bleed photo backdrop REMOVED (verified: 0 remaining). The scrim went
    with it.
  - The photograph now occupies ONE bento tile spanning the full left column,
    with its own local gradient confined to that tile.
  - Layout changed from a uniform 3-col grid to a 3x3 bento: one tall
    photographic tile plus six stat tiles. No longer a generic card row.
  - Cards changed from bg-white/[0.045] (near-transparent over a busy photo) to
    solid navy-700/75 with backdrop blur.
  - Body copy recoloured slatey-400 -> azure-200. Measured contrast against the
    card is now 11.04:1, comfortably past WCAG AAA.
  - Every feature body trimmed to a single short sentence; the section intro cut
    from two lines to one.
  - Stat moved up beside the icon so each tile leads with the number.
  - Stagger replaced with per-tile Reveal wrappers: a Stagger here would need
    display:contents, which leaves its IntersectionObserver without a layout
    box and stops it firing entirely.

## FOOTER

  Copyright year removed: now "© Aloha Fiber. All rights reserved."

  Verified after all changes at 1024px, full-page scroll:
    4/4 wipes open · 0 clipped · 0/40 reveals faded · 0 overflowing elements ·
    no horizontal scroll · build clean.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v1.5.0] — PHOTOGRAPHY LANDED AND OPTIMISED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  All five assets supplied and implemented. Two problems had to be fixed before
  they could ship.

  1. GENERATOR WATERMARK. Every image carried a four-pointed sparkle in the
     bottom-right corner. Pixel analysis on the darkest frame put its bounding
     box at x:1264-1366, y:625-724 in the 1408x768 source. All five are now
     cropped to 1250px wide, which removes it cleanly without touching the
     subject in any frame.

  2. WEIGHT. Delivered as PNG at 28.7 MB total (hero 6.75 MB, antenna 8.57 MB).
     That would have destroyed the PageSpeed budget the brief calls for.
     Converted to progressive mozjpeg, q82, 4:4:4 chroma:

       hero-kau-coastline   6.75 MB ->  70 KB   1250x768
       tower-ridge-dusk     7.68 MB ->  79 KB   1250x768
       antenna-install      8.57 MB -> 135 KB   1250x768
       home-connected       2.27 MB -> 178 KB   1250x768
       og-share             2.08 MB -> 110 KB   1200x630  (exact OG ratio)
       ------------------------------------------------
       TOTAL               28.70 MB -> 572 KB   (98% reduction)

     PNG originals moved to design/source-images/, OUTSIDE the served folder.
     Next copies /public wholesale into the build, so leaving them there would
     have shipped 28 MB of dead weight.

  Scrim rebalancing — the original scrims were tuned against gradients and
  buried the photographs once they were real:

    Hero      95/88/72% -> 93/82/46/28% across a 100deg axis, so the copy
              column stays dark while the coastline reads on the right. Added
              a bottom weight so the ticker edge stays clean. Decorative dot
              plane dropped 0.55 -> 0.22 and both glows softened, since the
              photograph now carries the depth.
    WhyAloha  94/90/95% -> 93/78/82/94%, letting the tower show through.
              Grid overlay dropped 0.05 -> 0.03.
    Coverage  Background position pinned to 72%/38% rather than centre: the
              banner crops to a 2.66:1 letterbox from a 1.63:1 source, and
              centring cut the technician out of frame.

  + design/IMAGE-ASSETS.md rewritten with the as-shipped sizes and a
    reproducible sharp command for regenerating any asset (sharp ships with
    Next, so no new dependency).

  Verified: all five serve HTTP 200 as image/jpeg, every layer resolves in the
  DOM, and og:image is populated.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v1.4.0] — CARD ICONOGRAPHY, PHOTOGRAPHY SLOTS, CTA LABEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## ICONS ADDED TO CARDS

  + components/ui/Icons.tsx — PlanGlyph, a three-bar signal ladder. Filled bars
    show the tier, faded bars the headroom above it, so Basic / Standard / Plus
    read as a progression at a glance rather than three identical cards.
    Driven by a new optional `icon` field on PlanItem ('signal-1|2|3').
  + IncludedIcon, OneTimeIcon, OptionalIcon for the fine-print groups, each
    with its own tint (azure / sunset / slate) matching the group's value
    colour.
  Plan card and fine-print card headers restructured to carry the glyph beside
  the title.

## CTA LABEL

  ZipChecker submit button: "Check" -> "Check availability".

## PHOTOGRAPHY — SLOTS WIRED, ASSETS PENDING

  New `media` export in lib/content.ts declares all five images in one place.
  Every slot is consumed as a CSS background layered UNDERNEATH the existing
  gradient/scrim, so a missing file degrades to the current gradient treatment
  instead of a broken image box. The site is safe to ship before the assets
  arrive, and no code change is needed when they land.

    hero        /images/hero-kau-coastline.jpg  2560x1440  hero, full bleed
    whyAloha    /images/tower-ridge-dusk.jpg    2400x1600  parallax backdrop
    coverage    /images/antenna-install.jpg     2000x1100  installation banner
    faq         /images/home-connected.jpg      1200x900   FAQ sidebar panel
    ogShare     /images/og-share.jpg            1200x630   Open Graph / Twitter

  The Why Aloha backdrop is bound to the existing Parallax primitive, which
  finally satisfies the brief's "parallax depth effects on background imagery"
  with real imagery rather than gradients.

  A new "Professional installation" banner was added at the head of the
  Coverage block to host the install photograph, carrying the $200 one-time
  setup charge in context.

  Open Graph and Twitter card images were previously absent from metadata and
  are now wired to ogShare.

  + design/IMAGE-ASSETS.md — filenames, sizes, placements, and the
    composition/weight rules (left third kept quiet for headline copy; assets
    sit under a dark scrim so they must be generated darker than feels right).


