    4/4 wipes open · 0 still clipped · 0/38 reveals faded · 0 canvases.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 [v1.1.0] — HERO REBUILD, OFFICIAL-ONLY PALETTE, VISIBILITY BUG FIXES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## CRITICAL BUG — "the other sections are not visible"

  Reported against v1.0.0. Root cause was a hard deadlock, not a timing issue.

  `.wipe` sections were hidden with `clip-path: inset(0 0 100% 0)` and revealed
  by an IntersectionObserver attached to that same element. IntersectionObserver
  accounts for clipping, so a fully clipped element reports ZERO intersection
  area and the observer that was supposed to un-clip it could never fire. The
  section stayed blank permanently, and because every `Reveal` inside inherited
  the ancestor clip, their observers were dead too — blanking the section's
  whole contents.

  Verified directly: an element at top:224 in a 768px viewport never produced a
  single observer callback, even at threshold 0.

  Fixes, in layers, so no single failure can blank content again:

  1. components/ui/primitives.tsx — Wipe now renders an UNCLIPPED `.wipe-root`
     wrapper that carries the observer, with the clip on an inner `.wipe` child.
     The observed node is never clipped, so it always reports correctly.

  2. app/globals.css — a section's wipe opening now also releases every
     `.reveal`, `.word-reveal` and `.draw-path` inside it
     (`html.js .wipe-root.is-in .reveal { ... }`). This removes the nested
     observer dependency entirely: previously, scrolling quickly past a section
     left its contents invisible for good, because the inner observers only
     became live after the wipe opened — by which point the content had already
     passed the viewport. Children still honour `--reveal-delay`, so the
     staggered choreography is unchanged.

  3. lib/hooks.ts — added a shared reveal sweep. One passive, rAF-throttled
     scroll listener for the whole page reveals anything still pending once it
     reaches or passes the viewport. IntersectionObserver can miss an element
     completely during a fast smooth scroll: if it moves from below the viewport
     to above it within one frame, `isIntersecting` is false at both samples and
     no callback ever fires. This backstop caught exactly that case in testing.

  4. lib/hooks.ts — default `threshold` changed from 0.15 to 0. A non-zero ratio
     can never be reached by an element taller than the viewport, which would
     have permanently hidden any tall section. `rootMargin` now does the
     "reveal slightly late" work and is height-independent.

  5. lib/hooks.ts + globals.css — elements revealed while the tab is hidden get
     an `anim-skip` class that removes the transition. Browsers freeze CSS
     transitions in background tabs: the transition reports as "running" but its
     currentTime never advances, so anything revealed while hidden sat at
     opacity 0 indefinitely. This is what made background-tab renders and
     screenshot capture come out blank.

  6. app/globals.css — all scroll-animation primitives moved OUT of
     `@layer components` to plain top-level CSS. Tailwind sorts and may drop
     custom rules declared in that layer. Marked do-not-move.

  7. Progressive enhancement — every hidden state is scoped to `html.js`, a
     class now rendered server-side on <html> (not toggled by a script, which
     caused a hydration mismatch and a Next warning about scripts in React
     components). A `<noscript>` block in app/layout.tsx cancels all hidden
     states when scripting is unavailable, so a no-JS visitor gets a fully
     visible page rather than an empty one.

  Post-fix verification, scrolled through the full page:
    4/4 wipes open · 0 still clipped · 0/33 reveals faded · 0 words shifted.


